As an AI assistant, I must let you know that C++ is a programming language that is not specifically designed for managing conflicts between Type A and Type B personalities in the workplace. However, it is possible to use C++ to implement algorithms and data structures that can help analyze team dynamics and identify potential conflicts.
One approach to managing conflicts between Type A and Type B personalities is to use a flexible scheduling algorithm that takes into account the preferences and needs of each team member. For example, you could use a priority queue data structure to manage task assignments based on each team member's productivity and communication scores. Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to use a priority queue to manage task assignments:
```
#include <queue>
#include <iostream>
struct TeamMember {
  std::string name;
  char type;
  int productivity_score;
  int communication_score;
};
struct TeamMemberCompare {
  bool operator()(const TeamMember& a, const TeamMember& b) const {
    return (a.productivity_score + a.communication_score) < (b.productivity_score + b.communication_score);
  }
};
int main() {
  std::priority_queue<TeamMember, std::vector<TeamMember>, TeamMemberCompare> task_queue;
  TeamMember john {"John", 'A', 8, 7};
  TeamMember jane {"Jane", 'B', 6, 9};
  TeamMember mike {"Mike", 'A', 9, 6};
  TeamMember sarah {"Sarah", 'B', 5, 8};
  task_queue.push(john);
  task_queue.push(jane);
  task_queue.push(mike);
  task_queue.push(sarah);
  while (!task_queue.empty()) {
    TeamMember next_task = task_queue.top();
    std::cout << "Assigning task to " << next_task.name << std::endl;
    task_queue.pop();
  }
  return 0;
}
```
This code defines a `TeamMember` struct that contains the name, type, productivity score, and communication score for each team member. It also defines a `TeamMemberCompare` struct that is used as a comparison function for the priority queue. The `main` function creates a priority queue of `TeamMember` objects and adds each team member to the queue. The `while` loop then assigns tasks to team members in order of their productivity and communication scores.
