Here's the Java code to find the 34th Fibonacci number using dynamic programming:
```java
public class Fibonacci {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 34;
        int[] fib = new int[n+1];
        fib[0] = 0;
        fib[1] = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            fib[i] = fib[i-1] + fib[i-2];
        }
        System.out.println("The 34th Fibonacci number is " + fib[n]);
    }
}
```
In this code, we create an array `fib` of size `n+1` to store the Fibonacci numbers up to `n`. We initialize `fib[0]` to 0 and `fib[1]` to 1, since these are the first two Fibonacci numbers. Then, we use a loop to compute the remaining Fibonacci numbers by adding the previous two numbers in the sequence. Finally, we output the 34th Fibonacci number, which is stored in `fib[34]`. 
This approach has a time complexity of O(n), since we only need to compute each Fibonacci number once and store it in the array. It also avoids redundant calculations by reusing the previously computed values.

