One innovation in the automotive industry is the use of autonomous vehicles. Autonomous vehicles are vehicles that are capable of driving themselves without human intervention. They use a combination of sensors, cameras, and machine learning algorithms to navigate roads and make decisions.
To describe how autonomous vehicles work in detail using Java code, we can create a simple simulation that models the behavior of an autonomous vehicle. Here is an example:
```
public class AutonomousVehicle {

  private double speed; // speed of the vehicle in km/h
  private double acceleration; // acceleration of the vehicle in m/s^2
  private double distanceToObstacle; // distance to the nearest obstacle in meters
  private boolean isObstacleDetected; // flag indicating whether an obstacle has been detected

  public AutonomousVehicle() {
    speed = 0;
    acceleration = 0;
    distanceToObstacle = Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
    isObstacleDetected = false;
  }

  public void update(double timeStep) {
    // update the speed and acceleration of the vehicle based on its current state
    speed += acceleration * timeStep;

    // if an obstacle is detected, adjust the acceleration to slow down the vehicle
    if (isObstacleDetected) {
      acceleration = -distanceToObstacle / (2 * timeStep * speed);
    } else {
      acceleration = 2; // default acceleration if no obstacle is detected
    }
  }

  public void detectObstacle(double distance) {
    // update the distance to the obstacle and set the flag indicating that an obstacle has been detected
    distanceToObstacle = distance;
    isObstacleDetected = true;
  }

  public void clearObstacle() {
    // reset the distance to the obstacle and clear the flag indicating that an obstacle has been detected
    distanceToObstacle = Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
    isObstacleDetected = false;
  }

  // getters and setters for the private variables
  public double getSpeed() {
    return speed;
  }

  public void setSpeed(double speed) {
    this.speed = speed;
  }

  public double getAcceleration() {
    return acceleration;
  }

  public void setAcceleration(double acceleration) {
    this.acceleration = acceleration;
  }

  public double getDistanceToObstacle() {
    return distanceToObstacle;
  }

  public void setDistanceToObstacle(double distanceToObstacle) {
    this.distanceToObstacle = distanceToObstacle;
  }

  public boolean isObstacleDetected() {
    return isObstacleDetected;
  }

  public void setObstacleDetected(boolean isObstacleDetected) {
    this.isObstacleDetected = isObstacleDetected;
  }
}
```
In this simulation, we have created a class called `AutonomousVehicle` that represents an autonomous vehicle. The class has four private variables: `speed`, `acceleration`, `distanceToObstacle`, and `isObstacleDetected`. 
The `update` method is responsible for updating the speed and acceleration of the vehicle based on its current state. If an obstacle is detected, the acceleration is adjusted to slow down the vehicle. If no obstacle is detected, the acceleration is set to a default value of 2 m/s^2.
The `detectObstacle` method is responsible for updating the distance to the nearest obstacle and setting the flag indicating that an obstacle has been detected.
The `clearObstacle` method is responsible for resetting the distance to the obstacle and clearing the flag indicating that an obstacle has been detected.
Finally, we have getters and setters for the private variables so that they can be accessed and modified from outside the class.
This simulation is a simplified example of how an autonomous vehicle might work in real life. In practice, autonomous vehicles use much more sophisticated algorithms and sensors to navigate roads and make decisions.

