To retrieve the definition of a given phrase and filter out any vulgar language or inappropriate content, you can use the Merriam-Webster Dictionary API. This API provides a wide range of definitions for words and phrases, and also includes a filter to exclude offensive content.
Here's a sample PHP code snippet that retrieves the definition of a given phrase and filters out any offensive content:
```php
$phrase = "kick the bucket";
$api_key = "YOUR_API_KEY"; // replace with your Merriam-Webster API key
// build the API request URL
$url = "https://www.dictionaryapi.com/api/v3/references/collegiate/json/{$phrase}?key={$api_key}";
// make the API request and decode the JSON response
$response = json_decode(file_get_contents($url), true);
// check if the response contains any definitions
if (isset($response[0]['shortdef'])) {
    // filter out any offensive content from the definitions
    $definitions = array_filter($response[0]['shortdef'], function($definition) {
        return !preg_match('/\b(vulgar|offensive|inappropriate)\b/i', $definition);
    });
    // output the filtered definitions
    echo implode(", ", $definitions);
} else {
    // handle the case where no definitions were found
    echo "No definitions found for '{$phrase}'";
}
```
In this code snippet, we first define the phrase we want to look up and our Merriam-Webster API key. We then build the API request URL using the phrase and API key, and make the API request using `file_get_contents()` and `json_decode()`.
Next, we check if the response contains any definitions by checking if the `shortdef` key is set in the first element of the response array. If there are definitions, we filter out any offensive content using `array_filter()` and a regular expression pattern that matches common offensive keywords. Finally, we output the filtered definitions using `implode()`.
If no definitions were found, we output a message indicating that no definitions were found.
