            {0, 0, 0, 4, 1, 9, 0, 0, 5},
            {0, 0, 0, 0, 8, 0, 0, 7, 9}
        };
        SudokuSolver solver = new SudokuSolver(board);
        System.out.println("Before solving:");
        solver.printBoard();
        solver.solve();
        System.out.println("After solving:");
        solver.printBoard();
    }
}
```
The `solve()` method uses a nested loop to iterate through each cell in the board. If a cell is empty, it tries each number from 1 to 9 to see if it is valid. If a number is valid, it sets the cell to that number and recursively calls `solve()` on the updated board. If `solve()` returns true, it means the puzzle has been solved and the method returns true. If `solve()` returns false, it means the current number was not valid and it tries the next number. If all numbers have been tried and none are valid, the method returns false.
The `isValid()` method checks if a number is valid for a given row, column, and 3x3 sub-grid. It first checks if the number is already in the same row or column. If it is, it returns false. It then calculates the starting row and column of the sub-grid that the cell belongs to and checks if the number is already in that sub-grid. If it is, it returns false. If the number is not in any of these locations, it returns true.
The `printBoard()` method simply prints out the current state of the board.
In the `main()` method, we create a new `SudokuSolver` object with the example puzzle and call `solve()` on it. We then print out the solved puzzle using `printBoard()`.

