To perform subtraction operation with a table of multiple input values and their corresponding outputs in C++, you can use a loop to iterate through the table and subtract the input values to get the output value. Here's a sample code that can handle input and output values of varying data types and formats:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    vector<vector<string>> table = {{"9", "6", "3"}, {"10.2", "4.8", "5.4"}, {"100", "33", "67"}, {"hello", "world", "Error"}};
    for (auto row : table) {
        try {
            if (row.size() != 3) {
                throw "Invalid table format";
            }
            if (row[0].find(".") != string::npos || row[1].find(".") != string::npos) {
                double input1 = stod(row[0]);
                double input2 = stod(row[1]);
                double output = input1 - input2;
                cout << input1 << " - " << input2 << " = " << output << endl;
            } else {
                int input1 = stoi(row[0]);
                int input2 = stoi(row[1]);
                int output = input1 - input2;
                cout << input1 << " - " << input2 << " = " << output << endl;
            }
        } catch (const char* msg) {
            cerr << msg << endl;
        } catch (const invalid_argument& e) {
            cerr << "Invalid input format" << endl;
        } catch (const out_of_range& e) {
            cerr << "Input value out of range" << endl;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define the table as a vector of vectors of strings. We then loop through each row of the table and perform the subtraction operation. We use a try-catch block to handle any invalid input or output values.
